      "text": "To find the measure of the smallest interior angle of a triangle where the angles are in the ratio of 5:6:7, we first need to determine the total number of parts that make up the sum of the interior angles of the triangle. The sum of the interior angles of any triangle is always 180 degrees.\n\nLet's denote the measures of the interior angles as \\(5x\\), \\(6x\\), and \\(7x\\) because they are in the ratio of 5:6:7. The sum of these angles is given by the equation:\n\\[\n5x + 6x + 7x = 180\n\\]\nCombining like terms, we get:\n\\[\n18x = 180\n\\]\nTo find \\(x\\), we divide both sides of the equation by 18:\n\\[\nx = \\frac{180}{18} = 10\n\\]\nNow that we have the value of \\(x\\), we can find the measure of each angle by substituting \\(x\\) back into the expressions for the angles:\n\\[\n5x = 5 \\times 10 = 50\n\\]\n\\[\n6x = 6 \\times 10 = 60\n\\]\n\\[\n7x = 7 \\times 10 = 70\n\\]\nThe smallest angle is the one corresponding to \\(5x\\), which is 50 degrees. Therefore, the measure of the smallest interior angle is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{50}\n\\]",
